At point B: exterior angle is 150° → interior angle at B = 30°

Then angle at C = 180° - 60° - 30° = 90°

That makes more sense — and now we have a 30-60-90 triangle!

And side AC = 17.5 — which is the side opposite the 30° angle? No.

Angles:

- ∠A = 60°
- ∠B = 30°
- ∠C = 90°

So side opposite ∠A (60°) is BC  
Side opposite ∠B (30°) is AC = 17.5  
Side opposite ∠C (90°) is AB

In a 30-60-90 triangle, sides are in ratio 1 : √3 : 2

Specifically:

- Side opposite 30° = shortest side = AC = 17.5
- Side opposite 60° = 17.5√3 = BC
- Side opposite 90° = 2 × 17.5 = 35 = AB

Therefore:

- AB = 35
- BC = 17.5√3
- AC = 17.5

Perimeter = AB + BC + AC = 35 + 17.5√3 + 17.5 = 52.5 + 17.5√3

Which is **option C**.

✅ So the correct interpretation is:

- The 120° at A is the **exterior** angle → interior angle at A = 60°
- The 150° at B is the **exterior** angle → interior angle at B = 30°
- Then angle at C = 90° → right triangle
- AC = 17.5 is opposite the 30° angle → so hypotenuse AB = 2 × 17.5 = 35
- BC = opposite 60° = 17.5√3

Perimeter = 35 + 17.5√3 + 17.5 = 52.5 + 17.5√3

